course: dictionary
lesson: 4186
title: Old words, old boots
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words are old-fashioned. You may find them in old books, where they are spelled differently from today. Golosh is an old British spelling of galosh. The sentence gives you clues: «His goloshes, which always lay by the umbrella-stand, were also gone.»
explain: The umbrella-stand is by the front door, and things kept there are things you use to go outside. The goloshes lay beside it, so they are probably shoes or boots for wet weather. Checking the meaning: a golosh is a rubber shoe worn over an ordinary shoe to keep it dry in rain or snow.
explain: «The goloshes were new, just bought for the winter, and the pimples on their soles were fresh and sharp.» New for winter tells you they were for cold, wet weather. Sharp soles mean the tracks were easy to read in the mud or snow. Good readers use every clue in a sentence, not just one.
fact: A golosh is a rubber overshoe worn over an ordinary shoe to keep it dry.
fact: Golosh is an old British spelling of galosh, which is the usual spelling today.
fact: Today children wear wellies (wellington boots) or rain boots instead of goloshes.
fact: In the US, galoshes is still sometimes used for rubber boots.
fact: When an old book uses an unusual spelling, look up the usual spelling in a dictionary.
exercise: What is a golosh? | A warm woolly sock worn in bed | A bag for carrying books to school | *A rubber shoe worn over an ordinary shoe to keep it dry | A type of umbrella with a long handle
exercise: In «The goloshes were new, just bought for the winter», what does winter tell you? | Goloshes were for the summer beach | *Goloshes were for cold, wet weather | Goloshes were for dancing | Goloshes were made of paper
exercise: Golosh is an old British spelling of which word? | gallon | gallows | galley | *galosh
test: What do children often wear today instead of goloshes on wet days? | *Wellies, or wellington boots | Umbrellas | Bowler hats | Sandals
test: Where did his goloshes always lay, according to the sentence? | In the bath | *By the umbrella-stand | Under the table | A bed
exam: Which statement is true? | Goloshes are used only in the US | A galosh is a type of hat | *In the US, galoshes is still sometimes used for rubber boots | Goloshes are made of wool
